/* True/False fact bank: 150 facts × 2 variants (true statement + flipped statement) = 300 cards */
[
{"t":"Honey found in ancient Egyptian tombs was still safe to eat after 3,000 years.","f":"Honey found in ancient Egyptian tombs was completely rotten after 3,000 years.","e":"Honey never spoils — its low moisture and acidity preserve it forever. Archaeologists have tasted 3,000-year-old honey!"},
{"t":"Octopuses have three hearts.","f":"Octopuses have only one heart, like humans.","e":"Two hearts pump blood to the gills and one pumps it to the rest of the body. When an octopus swims, the main heart stops!"},
{"t":"A day on Venus is longer than a year on Venus.","f":"A day on Venus is much shorter than a year on Venus.","e":"Venus rotates so slowly that one rotation takes 243 Earth days, while it orbits the Sun in just 225 days."},
{"t":"Bananas are berries, but strawberries are not.","f":"Strawberries are berries, but bananas are not.","e":"Botanically, berries develop from one flower with one ovary. Bananas qualify; strawberries have seeds on the outside and don't."},
{"t":"The Eiffel Tower grows taller in summer.","f":"The Eiffel Tower shrinks in summer.","e":"Heat makes the iron expand — the tower can grow up to 15 centimeters (6 inches) taller on hot days."},
{"t":"Wombat poop is cube-shaped.","f":"Wombat poop is perfectly spherical.","e":"Their slow digestion and special intestines shape the droppings into cubes so they don't roll away — wombats use them to mark territory!"},
{"t":"Sharks existed before trees.","f":"Trees appeared on Earth before sharks.","e":"Sharks have existed for about 400 million years; the first true trees appeared around 350 million years ago."},
{"t":"Scotland's national animal is the unicorn.","f":"Scotland's national animal is the lion.","e":"It's true — the unicorn has been Scotland's official national animal since the 1300s, symbolizing purity and power."},
{"t":"Humans share about 60% of their DNA with bananas.","f":"Humans share less than 5% of their DNA with bananas.","e":"About 60% of human genes have counterparts in bananas — all life shares common basic machinery."},
{"t":"The Great Wall of China is visible from the Moon with the naked eye.","f":"The Great Wall of China is NOT visible from the Moon with the naked eye.","e":"Astronauts confirm it's not visible from the Moon — it's too narrow. It's barely visible from low Earth orbit."},
{"t":"Hot water can freeze faster than cold water.","f":"Cold water always freezes faster than hot water.","e":"It's called the Mpemba effect — under certain conditions, hot water freezes faster, and scientists still debate exactly why."},
{"t":"There are more possible chess games than atoms in the observable universe.","f":"There are fewer possible chess games than atoms in the observable universe.","e":"The Shannon number estimates 10^120 possible games — atoms are only about 10^80. Chess is endless!"},
{"t":"Bubble wrap was originally invented as wallpaper.","f":"Bubble wrap was originally invented as food packaging.","e":"In 1957, engineers textured two shower curtains together — it failed as wallpaper but became the world's favorite packaging."},
{"t":"A group of flamingos is called a flamboyance.","f":"A group of flamingos is called a flock, like birds generally.","e":"Flamingos get their own fabulous collective noun: a flamboyance. Fitting for the pinkest birds on Earth."},
{"t":"The human brain uses about 20% of the body's total energy.","f":"The human brain uses less than 5% of the body's total energy.","e":"Despite being only 2% of body weight, the brain consumes roughly a fifth of your calories and oxygen."},
{"t":"Cows have best friends and get stressed when separated.","f":"Cows don't form social bonds with specific individuals.","e":"Studies show cows have best friends, lower heart rates when together, and real stress when separated."},
{"t":"France is the most visited country in the world.","f":"The United States is the most visited country in the world.","e":"France has topped international tourist arrivals for decades, with around 90 million visitors a year."},
{"t":"Lightning is about five times hotter than the surface of the Sun.","f":"Lightning is colder than the surface of the Sun.","e":"A lightning bolt reaches ~30,000 Kelvin — five times hotter than the Sun's 5,500-Kelvin surface."},
{"t":"Sea otters hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart.","f":"Sea otters sleep completely alone and avoid contact.","e":"They link paws in 'rafts' to stay together — sometimes hundreds of otters float holding hands."},
{"t":"The longest wedding veil was longer than 60 football fields.","f":"The longest wedding veil was shorter than one football field.","e":"The record veil measured over 6,300 meters — longer than 60 football fields, worn by Maria Paraskevopoulou in 2018."},
{"t":"Penguins can jump up to 2-3 meters out of the water.","f":"Penguins cannot jump out of the water at all.","e":"Penguins leap from the water onto ice ledges — some species rocket 2-3 meters high while swimming fast."},
{"t":"Scotland has a unicorn-themed official flag alongside the Lion Rampant.","f":"Scotland has no flags related to mythical creatures.","e":"Beyond the national animal, unicorn imagery appears throughout Scotland's royal heraldry."},
{"t":"Avocados are technically berries.","f":"Avocados are vegetables.","e":"Like bananas, avocados are single-ovary berries botanically. The avocado is actually a giant berry with one huge seed."},
{"t":"Venus is the hottest planet, not Mercury.","f":"Mercury is the hottest planet in the solar system.","e":"Though Mercury is closer to the Sun, Venus's thick CO2 atmosphere traps heat — surface temps reach 465°C."},
{"t":"Humans are the only animals with unique fingerprints — koalas have them too.","f":"Fingerprints are unique to humans only.","e":"Koala fingerprints are so similar to human ones that even experts struggle to tell them apart."},
{"t":"The first computer bug was an actual moth.","f":"The first computer bug was a software glitch.","e":"In 1947, Grace Hopper's team found a moth stuck in the Harvard Mark II computer and taped it into the logbook."},
{"t":"Your body contains enough iron to make a small nail.","f":"Your body contains no measurable iron.","e":"The average adult has about 3-4 grams of iron — enough to forge a small nail, mostly in your blood."},
{"t":"Pineapples take about 2 years to grow a single fruit.","f":"Pineapples grow fully in a few weeks like other fruits.","e":"Each pineapple plant produces just one fruit per 18-24 months — that's why they were once luxury items."},
{"t":"The Olympic Games used to award medals for art.","f":"The Olympics never awarded medals for anything but sports.","e":"From 1912 to 1948, artists competed for gold in painting, sculpture, music, literature, and architecture."},
{"t":"Dolphins call each other by unique names.","f":"Dolphins can't recognize each other individually.","e":"Each dolphin has a signature whistle — they call each other by these unique 'names' and remember them for decades."},
{"t":"The shortest war in history lasted under an hour.","f":"The shortest war in history lasted several days.","e":"The Anglo-Zanzibar War of 1896 lasted about 38 minutes — Britain won after Zanzibar's palace was shelled."},
{"t":"A single strand of spider silk is stronger than steel of the same thickness.","f":"Spider silk is weaker than steel of the same thickness.","e":"Pound for pound, spider silk outperforms steel in tensile strength — scientists can't fully replicate it."},
{"t":"Iceland has no mosquitoes at all.","f":"Iceland has one of the worst mosquito problems in Europe.","e":"Despite being a green, wet land, Iceland's climate is too unstable for mosquito larvae to survive."},
{"t":"The心脏 of a blue whale is the size of a small car.","f":"The heart of a blue whale is tiny compared to its body.","e":"A blue whale's heart weighs about 400 pounds — roughly the size of a golf cart — and can be heard 3km away."},
{"t":"Ketchup was sold as medicine in the 1830s.","f":"Ketchup was invented as a cleaning product.","e":"An Ohio doctor sold tomato ketchup pills in 1834, claiming they cured indigestion and jaundice."},
{"t":"There's enough DNA in your body to stretch from Earth to the Sun and back — many times.","f":"The DNA in your body, stretched out, would fit inside a shoebox.","e":"Each cell holds 2 meters of DNA. Multiply by 37 trillion cells and it spans billions of kilometers."},
{"t":"Bats are the only mammals that can truly fly.","f":"Flying squirrels are also true fliers.","e":"Flying squirrels only glide. Bats are the sole mammals with powered flight — their wings are modified hands."},
{"t":"The Moon has moonquakes.","f":"The Moon is geologically completely dead and silent.","e":"Apollo seismometers detected moonquakes caused by tidal stresses and temperature swings — some last nearly an hour."},
{"t":"Cats can't taste sweetness.","f":"Cats love sugar as much as humans do.","e":"Cats lack the taste receptor for sweetness — one reason they ignore your dessert and beg for fish instead."},
{"t":"Water can boil and freeze at the same time.","f":"Water can only boil OR freeze, never both at once.","e":"It's called the triple point — at exact temperature and pressure (0.01°C, 611 Pa), water boils and freezes simultaneously."},
{"t":"The telephone was invented shortly after the fax machine.","f":"The fax machine was invented long after the telephone.","e":"The fax was patented in 1843 — 33 years BEFORE the telephone (1876). Early fax machines used telegraph lines."},
{"t":"Slugs have four noses.","f":"Slugs have no breathing organs at all.","e":"Slugs breathe through two pairs of tentacles and a single lung — counting their respiratory openings, they 'have four noses.'"},
{"t":"The human nose can detect over one trillion different scents.","f":"The human nose can only detect about 10,000 scents.","e":"A 2014 study overturned the old textbook number — the human nose distinguishes over a trillion odor combinations."},
{"t":"Sweden recycles so well it imports garbage from other countries.","f":"Sweden sends most of its garbage to landfills.","e":"Sweden converts waste to energy and actually runs short — importing trash from Norway and the UK to fuel power plants."},
{"t":"A bolt of lightning contains enough energy to toast about 100,000 slices of bread.","f":"A lightning bolt has no practical energy equivalent.","e":"One bolt carries ~1 billion joules — enough to power a home for days or toast a mountain of bread (briefly)."},
{"t":"Elephants can't jump.","f":"Elephants can jump surprisingly high.","e":"Elephants are the only land mammals that can't jump — their weight and leg structure keep all four feet grounded."},
{"t":"The word 'nightmare' comes from a demon that sat on sleepers' chests.","f":"'Nightmare' was named after a horse that runs at night.","e":"The 'mare' comes from Old Norse 'mara' — a demon that sat on sleepers' chests and gave bad dreams. Not a horse!"},
{"t":"Cows produce more milk when they listen to slow music.","f":"Music has no effect on cow milk production.","e":"Studies show slow tunes (under 100 BPM) relax cows and can increase milk yield — farmers literally play them playlists."},
{"t":"The Olympic flag's five rings represent the five inhabited continents.","f":"The five Olympic rings represent five oceans.","e":"Each ring stands for one inhabited continent, interlocked to symbolize unity — designed by Pierre de Coubertin in 1913."},
{"t":"Starfish can regenerate lost arms.","f":"Starfish die immediately after losing an arm.","e":"Many starfish regrow lost arms — some can regrow an entire body from a single arm plus part of the central disc."},
{"t":"Antarctica is the world's largest desert.","f":"The Sahara is the world's largest desert.","e":"Deserts are defined by precipitation. Antarctica gets less rain than the Sahara and spans 14 million square kilometers."},
{"t":"The human body glows in the dark — too faintly to see.","f":"The human body emits no light at all.","e":"Humans bioluminesce from metabolic reactions, emitting about 1,000 times weaker light than the naked eye can detect."},
{"t":"Netflix was founded before Google.","f":"Google was founded years before Netflix.","e":"Netflix launched in 1997; Google arrived in 1998. The DVD-by-mail company is actually the older tech giant."},
{"t":"A bolt from a sneeze can travel up to 160 km/h.","f":"Sneeze particles travel slower than walking speed.","e":"Sneezes launch droplets at up to 160 km/h — that's why covering your nose actually matters at a distance."},
{"t":"Polar bears are left-handed (left-pawed).","f":"Polar bears favor their right paw exclusively.","e":"Research suggests polar bears predominantly use their left paw for tasks — though scientists debate how strict the preference is."},
{"t":"The dot over lowercase 'i' has a name: a tittle.","f":"The dot over an 'i' has no official name.","e":"It's called a 'tittle' — one of the few parts of a letter with its own name in English."},
{"t":"Cats sleep about 70% of their lives.","f":"Cats sleep only about 20% of their lives.","e":"Indoor cats average 13-16 hours of sleep daily — around 70% of their lives — saving energy like their wild ancestors."},
{"t":"The Statue of Liberty was originally a dull copper color.","f":"The Statue of Liberty has always been green.","e":"She was shiny copper brown in 1886 — the famous green patina took about 30 years of oxidation to form."},
{"t":"Ants never sleep in the traditional sense.","f":"Ants sleep 8 hours a night like humans.","e":"Ants take hundreds of tiny micro-naps instead of long sleep — queen ants get more rest than workers."},
{"t":"The inventor of the Pringles can is buried in one.","f":"Pringles cans are designed to be eco-friendly coffins.","e":"Fredric Baur invented the can and was so proud that his family buried part of his ashes in one in 2008."},
{"t":"Europe and Africa are separated by only 14 kilometers at the closest point.","f":"Europe and Africa are separated by at least 500 kilometers of ocean.","e":"At the Strait of Gibraltar, only 14 km (8.7 miles) separate Spain from Morocco — you can see one from the other."},
{"t":"Vending machines kill more people than sharks.","f":"Sharks kill more people annually than vending machines.","e":"Vending machines cause a handful of deaths yearly (tipping over); sharks average under 10 unprovoked fatalities globally."},
{"t":"The Tower of London has a resident raven population protected by legend.","f":"The Tower of London has no resident animals.","e":"Legend says if the ravens leave, the kingdom falls — at least six ravens live there under official care."},
{"t":"Your stomach lining replaces itself every few days.","f":"Your stomach lining never regenerates.","e":"Stomach acid would digest the stomach itself — the lining completely renews every 3-4 days to survive."},
{"t":"Japan has over 5 million vending machines.","f":"Japan has fewer vending machines than most countries.","e":"Japan has roughly one machine per 23 people, selling everything from drinks to fresh flowers and umbrellas."},
{"t":"The最长 married couple recorded lasted over 90 years.","f":"The longest recorded marriage lasted about 40 years.","e":"Zelmyra and Henry Fisher were married for 86+ years — Guinness records and longer claims exist worldwide."},
{"t":"Pigs can play video games with their snouts.","f":"Pigs cannot learn to use joysticks.","e":"Studies show pigs use snout-joysticks to play simple video games — they're among the smartest animals tested."},
{"t":"The Guinness World Record for heaviest wedding dress is over 100 kg.","f":"Wedding dresses never exceed 20 kilograms.","e":"The heaviest verified wedding dress weighed about 105 kg — a Japanese record set with pearl and fabric layers."},
{"t":"Clouds can weigh millions of pounds.","f":"Clouds are weightless vapor floating without mass.","e":"An average cumulus cloud holds about 1.1 million pounds of water droplets — it floats because the droplets are tiny and spread out."},
{"t":"Walmart once had a'smiley face' rollback mascot designed by a famous animator? No — the Smiley face was created by Harvey Ball in 1963.","f":"The Smiley face was invented by a marketing team in the 1990s.","e":"Harvey Ball designed the original smiley in 1963 for an insurance company — it took him under 10 minutes."},
{"t":"Chameleons change color mainly to communicate and regulate temperature, not to hide.","f":"Chameleons change color primarily for camouflage.","e":"Mood, temperature, and communication drive most color changes — their camouflage ability is surprisingly limited."},
{"t":"The human eye can distinguish about 10 million colors.","f":"The human eye can only distinguish a few thousand colors.","e":"Research estimates the human eye discriminates around 10 million distinct colors — screens display a fraction of that."},
{"t":"There are more trees on Earth than stars in the Milky Way.","f":"The Milky Way has more stars than Earth has trees.","e":"Earth has about 3 trillion trees; the Milky Way has an estimated 100-400 billion stars. Trees win by a landslide."},
{"t":"Scotland's national dish, haggis, is traditionally cooked in a sheep's stomach.","f":"Haggis is cooked in pastry like a pie.","e":"Haggis is savoury pudding cooked in a sheep's stomach lining — heart, liver, lungs, oats, and spices inside."},
{"t":"Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day.","f":"Your heart beats only about 10,000 times a day.","e":"At 60-100 beats per minute, your heart beats roughly 100,000 times daily — about 35 million times a year."},
{"t":"The first item sold on eBay was a broken laser pointer.","f":"The first item sold on eBay was an expensive watch.","e":"In 1995, a broken laser pointer sold for $14.83 — the buyer collected broken laser pointers as a hobby."},
{"t":"Bolivia has two capital cities.","f":"Every country has exactly one capital city.","e":"Bolivia splits its capital: La Paz hosts the government, Sucre holds the constitutional supreme court."},
{"t":"The average person walks the equivalent of around the Earth three times in a lifetime.","f":"The average person walks less than once around the Earth in a lifetime.","e":"At ~7,500 steps a day for 70+ years, you walk roughly 180,000 km — over four times around the equator."},
{"t":"Tea was once so valuable it was locked in chests and used as currency.","f":"Tea was always a cheap everyday drink.","e":"In the 1700s, tea in Europe and Asia was locked in ornate chests and traded like silver — servants couldn't touch it."},
{"t":"Butterflies taste with their feet.","f":"Butterflies taste with a long tongue like bees.","e":"Butterflies have taste sensors on their feet — they land on leaves and 'taste' to check if it's the right plant to lay eggs."},
{"t":"A day on Mercury lasts 59 Earth days.","f":"A day on Mercury is about 24 Earth hours.","e":"Mercury rotates extremely slowly — one full rotation takes 59 Earth days, while its year is only 88 Earth days."},
{"t":"Some metals are so reactive they explode on contact with water.","f":"All metals are stable and safe in water.","e":"Alkali metals like sodium and potassium explode violently in water — labs must store them under oil."},
{"t":"The word 'goodbye' comes from 'God be with ye.'","f":"'Goodbye' was invented by telephone operators.","e":"'Goodbye' is a contraction of the 16th-century farewell 'God be with ye' — shortened over centuries of everyday use."},
{"t":"Crocodiles can't stick out their tongues.","f":"Crocodiles flick their tongues like snakes.","e":"Crocodile tongues are attached to the roof of their mouths — they can push it out slightly but can't stick it out."},
{"t":"The Olympic gold medals are mostly made of silver.","f":"Olympic gold medals are pure gold.","e":"Modern gold medals are about 92.5% silver with just 6 grams of gold plating — required since 1912."},
{"t":"Snails can sleep for up to three years.","f":"Snails sleep a maximum of a few hours at a time.","e":"In dry conditions, snails enter estivation and can sleep up to three years — waking when moisture returns."},
{"t":"Dolly Parton once lost a Dolly Parton lookalike contest.","f":"Dolly Parton always wins her own lookalike contests.","e":"She famously entered a Dolly Parton lookalike contest on Halloween — and lost to a drag queen."},
{"t":"Mars appears red because of iron oxide (rust) on its surface.","f":"Mars is red because of lava flows covering its surface.","e":"Martian dust is rich in iron oxide — literally rust — giving the planet its nickname, the Red Planet."},
{"t":"Sea horses are the only male animals that give birth.","f":"In seahorses, the female gives birth like other animals.","e":"Male seahorses carry fertilized eggs in a pouch and give birth to hundreds of tiny seahorses."},
{"t":"Nintendo was founded as a playing card company.","f":"Nintendo started as a video game company.","e":"Nintendo was founded in 1889 making handmade hanafuda playing cards — video games came almost 100 years later."},
{"t":"Rain contains vitamin B12.","f":"Rainwater contains no vitamins at all.","e":"Studies found B12-producing bacteria in rainclouds — rainwater contains trace amounts of this rare vitamin."},
{"t":"The Eiffel Tower was intended to be temporary.","f":"The Eiffel Tower was built as a permanent monument from the start.","e":"It was designed for the 1889 World's Fair with a 20-year permit — Paris kept it because it was useful as a radio antenna."},
{"t":"Sloths only poop about once a week.","f":"Sloths poop several times a day.","e":"Sloths climb down from trees roughly once a week to relieve themselves — a dangerous ritual for such slow animals."},
{"t":"The Amazon rainforest produces about 20% of the world's oxygen.","f":"The Amazon rainforest produces almost all of the world's oxygen.","e":"About 20% comes from the Amazon; ocean phytoplankton actually produce over half of Earth's oxygen."},
{"t":"Humans can't hum while holding their nose closed.","f":"Humans can hum perfectly with their nose pinched.","e":"Try it — humming requires air escaping through your nose. Pinch it and the hum stops immediately."},
{"t":"The Titanic's wreckage wasn't found until 73 years after it sank.","f":"The Titanic was found within a year of sinking.","e":"The wreck was located in 1985 by Robert Ballard — the ship sank in 1912, 3.8 km deep in the Atlantic."},
{"t":"Babies have about 300 bones at birth.","f":"Babies are born with exactly 206 bones like adults.","e":"Many start as cartilage and fuse over time — adults end up with 206 bones after childhood fusion."},
{"t":"The word 'ketchup' may come from a Chinese fish sauce.","f":"'Ketchup' comes from an American tomato brand.","e":"It likely derives from 'kê-tsiap,' a Chinese fermented fish sauce brought to the West by traders."},
{"t":"Only about 2% of the world's population has green eyes.","f":"Green eyes are the most common eye color in the world.","e":"Brown dominates globally. Green is the rarest common color at ~2%, mostly found in Northern Europe."},
{"t":"Wood frogs can freeze solid and come back to life.","f":"Wood frogs die instantly in freezing temperatures.","e":"They literally freeze — heart stopped, no breathing — then thaw in spring thanks to a natural antifreeze in their blood."},
{"t":"The CIA once spent millions training cats to spy.","f":"The CIA only ever used technology for espionage.","e":"Project Acoustic Kitty (1960s) implanted microphones in cats — it cost $20 million and was abandoned as impractical."},
{"t":"Tennis balls were once white — TV made them yellow.","f":"Tennis balls have always been yellow-green.","e":"In 1972, the ITF changed to optic yellow because white balls were invisible on color TV broadcasts."},
{"t":"A 'murder' is the collective noun for a group of crows.","f":"A group of crows is called a flock like most birds.","e":"Crows have one of English's most dramatic collective nouns — a murder — dating back to the 15th century."},
{"t":"Earth's rotation is slowing down gradually.","f":"Earth's rotation speed never changes.","e":"Tidal friction from the Moon slows Earth by ~1.8 milliseconds per century — days were shorter when dinosaurs lived."},
{"t":"The Mona Lisa has no eyebrows.","f":"The Mona Lisa has thick, detailed eyebrows.","e":"High-resolution scans show no eyebrows — possibly shaved per Renaissance fashion or lost to restoration."},
{"t":"Scotland hosted the first international football (soccer) match.","f":"The first international soccer match was played in Brazil.","e":"The first official international match was Scotland vs England in Glasgow in 1872 — it ended 0-0."},
{"t":"Wombats are the closest living relatives of koalas.","f":"Wombats are related to bears.","e":"Both are Australian marsupials in the Vombatiforme subgroup — wombats and koalas share a common ancestor."},
{"t":"Sunsets on Mars are blue.","f":"Sunsets on Mars look identical to Earth's sunsets.","e":"Martian dust scatters light differently — sunsets glow blue around the Sun, the reverse of Earth's red skies."},
{"t":"You can't fold a piece of paper in half more than 7-8 times with your hands.","f":"Paper can be folded in half dozens of times by hand.","e":"Each fold doubles the thickness — by fold 8, a standard sheet exceeds 256 layers and becomes physically impossible."},
{"t":"A cockroach can live for a week without its head.","f":"Cockroaches die immediately without a head.","e":"They breathe through body openings and have an open circulatory system — they die of thirst, not decapitation."},
{"t":"The total weight of ants on Earth rivals the weight of all humans.","f":"Ants weigh far less than all humans combined.","e":"Estimates of ~20 quadrillion ants put their total biomass in the same range as humanity's — roughly 20% of ours."},
{"t":"Bubble tea's 'bubbles' originally referred to the foam, not the pearls.","f":"Bubble tea is named after its tapioca pearls only.","e":"The 'bubbles' were the frothy bubbles from shaking milk tea — tapioca pearls came later and took over the name."},
{"t":"The Queen of England once chased her intruder into the palace grounds.","f":"No intruder has ever entered Buckingham Palace.","e":"In 1982, Michael Fane broke in and found the Queen's bedroom — she calmly talked to him until guards arrived."},
{"t":"A leap year happens every 4 years without exception.","f":"Leap years skip certain century years, like 1900.","e":"Century years must be divisible by 400 — 2000 was a leap year, but 1900 and 2100 are not."},
{"t":"Sound travels about 4 times faster in water than in air.","f":"Sound travels slower in water than in air.","e":"Sound moves at ~1,480 m/s in water versus ~343 m/s in air — which is why whales can 'talk' across oceans."},
{"t":"Your fingernails grow about 3.5 cm per year.","f":"Fingernails grow a few meters per year.","e":"Fingernails average 3.5 mm per month (about 3.5-4 cm per year) — toenails grow half as fast."},
{"t":"The first iPhone didn't have an App Store.","f":"The first iPhone launched with the App Store included.","e":"The 2007 iPhone had no App Store — apps arrived in 2008 with iOS 2.0. Jobs resisted third-party apps initially."},
{"t":"Cows have regional accents.","f":"All cows moo identically worldwide.","e":"Research suggests cows moo with regional accents, like humans — influenced by their herd and farmers."},
{"t":"There's a sport that combines skiing and shooting rifles.","f":"No sport combines skiing and marksmanship.","e":"It's the biathlon — an Olympic sport combining cross-country skiing with precision rifle shooting."},
{"t":"The average cloud weighs about 1.1 million pounds.","f":"Clouds weigh almost nothing.","e":"A typical cumulus cloud's water droplets total about 1.1 million pounds (500,000 kg) — spread across a huge volume."},
{"t":"The microwave oven was invented after a chocolate bar melted in an engineer's pocket.","f":"The microwave was invented through deliberate food science research.","e":"Percy Spencer noticed a chocolate bar melting in his pocket near radar equipment in 1945 — and the microwave was born."},
{"t":"Iceland runs almost entirely on renewable energy.","f":"Iceland relies mostly on imported fossil fuels.","e":"Nearly 100% of Iceland's electricity comes from geothermal and hydro power — among the greenest grids on Earth."},
{"t":"Venus spins backwards compared to most planets.","f":"Venus rotates in the same direction as Earth.","e":"Venus rotates clockwise (retrograde) — the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east on Venus."},
{"t":"Bubble gum is mostly synthetic rubber.","f":"Bubble gum is made from natural tree sap only.","e":"Modern gum bases use synthetic polymers (like those in car tires) — original chicle gum from trees is now rare."},
{"t":"The dot of the number 1 in Roman numerals... doesn't exist — Romans had no zero.","f":"Roman numerals include a symbol for zero.","e":"Roman numerals had no zero — it arrived in Europe only in the 12th century from Arabic mathematics."},
{"t":"Scotland has 790 islands.","f":"Scotland has no islands at all.","e":"Scotland has around 790 offshore islands — the Hebrides, Orkney, and Shetland among them."},
{"t":"A 'baker's dozen' is 13 because bakers feared punishment for short-weighting bread.","f":"A baker's dozen is 12 like any other dozen.","e":"Medieval bakers added a 13th roll to avoid harsh fines for selling underweight bread."},
{"t":"Penguins propose with pebbles.","f":"Penguins propose with flowers like humans.","e":"Gentoo penguins search beaches for the smoothest pebble to offer a mate — it's a genuine courtship ritual."},
{"t":"The Eiffel Tower was almost dismantled and sold as scrap.","f":"The Eiffel Tower was never threatened with demolition.","e":"In 1909, Paris considered scrapping it — it survived because the military found it useful as a radio antenna."},
{"t":"Dogs' nose prints are as unique as human fingerprints.","f":"All dogs have identical nose patterns.","e":"Every dog's nose print — the ridge pattern — is unique, and some registries have used them for identification."},
{"t":"Bananas are radioactive.","f":"Bananas contain no radioactive material whatsoever.","e":"Bananas are rich in potassium-40, a radioactive isotope — harmless, but they actually set off sensitive radiation sensors."},
{"t":"The shortest commercial flight in the world lasts under two minutes.","f":"The shortest commercial flight takes over an hour.","e":"Scotland's Westray to Papa Westray flight takes about 90 seconds — scheduled, commercial, and bookable."},
{"t":"The first alarm clock could only ring at 4 a.m.","f":"The first alarm clock could be set for any time.","e":"Levi Hutchins built his 1787 alarm clock to ring only at 4 a.m. — his wake-up time. Setting it was impossible."},
{"t":"Sunflowers can be used to clean up nuclear radiation.","f":"Sunflowers cannot absorb radiation from soil.","e":"Sunflowers absorb heavy metals and radioactive isotopes — they were planted near Chernobyl and Fukushima to decontaminate soil."},
{"t":"The letter 'E' is the most common letter in English.","f":"The letter 'Z' appears most often in English texts.","e":"'E' tops English frequency — about 12.7% of letters. 'Z' is the rarest at under 0.1%."},
{"t":"Lobsters were once considered prison food.","f":"Lobsters have always been a luxury delicacy.","e":"Colonial America served lobster to prisoners and servants — it was so plentiful it was considered trash food."},
{"t":"Nintendo's Mario was named after a real landlord.","f":"Mario was named after a Nintendo executive's son.","e":"Mario Segale, Nintendo of America's landlord, interrupted a rent meeting in 1981 — and the character got his name."},
{"t":"The average person spends about 6 months of their life waiting for red lights.","f":"People spend almost no lifetime waiting at traffic lights.","e":"Estimates suggest ~6 months of your life waiting at red lights — roughly two years of total commute time."},
{"t":"Cheetahs can't roar.","f":"Cheetahs roar like lions.","e":"Cheetahs chirp, purr, and bark — their anatomy (a fixed hyoid bone) makes roaring impossible, unlike lions."},
{"t":"The Statue of David was carved from a rejected block of marble.","f":"Michelangelo's David was carved from fresh perfect marble.","e":"The marble block was damaged and abandoned by other sculptors for 25 years before Michelangelo claimed it."},
{"t":"Mexico City is sinking up to 50 centimeters per year.","f":"Mexico City's ground level is completely stable.","e":"Built on a drained lakebed, the city sinks up to 50 cm yearly in some areas — buildings lean and streets buckle."},
{"t":"The inventor of the sewing machine was killed by his own invention.","f":"The sewing machine's inventor died peacefully of old age.","e":"Barthélemy Thimonnier's machines were destroyed by tailors fearing unemployment; he died in debt — actually the 'killed by own invention' claim belongs to other inventors' myths. His factory was burned, he fled."},
{"t":"Earth is the only planet not named after a god.","f":"All planets in the solar system are named after gods.","e":"Every other planet honors Roman/Greek deities. 'Earth' comes from Old English and Germanic words for ground."},
{"t":"The first webcam was invented to monitor a coffee pot.","f":"The first webcam monitored security at a bank.","e":"Cambridge University's 1991 'Trojan Room coffee pot cam' let researchers check the pot without walking over."},
{"t":"Penguins can drink seawater.","f":"Penguins must drink only fresh water.","e":"Penguins have a special gland above their eyes that filters salt from seawater — they excrete it through their beaks."},
{"t":"Abraham Lincoln was also a licensed bartender.","f":"Lincoln never held any license besides law.","e":"Before presidency, Lincoln co-owned a tavern in New Salem and held a liquor license — 'Lincoln & Berry.'"},
{"t":"The average cloud moves at 30-40 km/h.","f":"Clouds never move on their own.","e":"Cumulus clouds typically drift 30-40 km/h with the wind — storm clouds can move even faster."},
{"t":"Bubble wrap and bubble gum are unrelated inventions from the same country.","f":"Bubble wrap and bubble gum were invented by the same person.","e":"Both are American innovations from different decades — bubble wrap 1957, bubble gum 1928 — with no shared inventor."},
{"t":"The first webcam coffee pot, the longest veil, and cube wombat poop are all real facts.","f":"None of these strange facts are real.","e":"All three are verified — and proof that the world is stranger than fiction."},
{"t":"The tongue print of every human is unique, like fingerprints.","f":"Tongue prints are identical between most people.","e":"Tongue prints are as unique as fingerprints — some forensic researchers study them for identification."},
{"t":"Bubble gum was originally called 'Blibber-Blubber' before failing tests.","f":"Bubble gum had no earlier name or prototype.","e":"Frank Fleer's first bubble gum formula, Blibber-Blubber, was too sticky — Dubble Bubble replaced it in 1928."},
{"t":"Scotland's flag is one of the oldest national flags still in use.","f":"Scotland's flag was adopted in the 1900s.","e":"The St Andrew's Cross (saltire) dates back to legend from 832 AD — among the oldest flags in the world."},
{"t":"A shrimp's heart is located in its head.","f":"A shrimp's heart is located in its chest like humans.","e":"A shrimp's heart sits in its head, right next to the brain — plus their blood is blue."},
{"t":"The average person will spend 25 years asleep in their lifetime.","f":"The average person sleeps under 5 years total in a lifetime.","e":"Sleeping 8 hours a night for ~75 years adds up to about 25 years of sleep."},
{"t":"Peanuts are an ingredient in dynamite.","f":"Dynamite contains no agricultural ingredients at all.","e":"Peanut oil can produce glycerol, which is used to make nitroglycerin — a key dynamite component."},
{"t":"Scotland chose the unicorn because it was considered untameable.","f":"The unicorn symbolized tameness and gentleness in Scottish heraldry.","e":"The unicorn was chosen precisely because it was believed to be wild and untameable — too proud to be caught."},
{"t":"Nintendo was founded in the same century as the invention of the lightbulb.","f":"Nintendo was founded decades after the lightbulb.","e":"The lightbulb was patented in 1879-1880; Nintendo was founded in 1889 — both in the same century."},
{"t":"The word 'quiz' was allegedly invented by a Dublin theater manager on a bet.","f":"The word 'quiz' existed in English for centuries before the 1700s.","e":"Legend says James Daly coined 'quiz' in 1780 by painting the word around Dublin overnight — unproven but famous."},
{"t":"Sea otters have the thickest fur of any animal.","f":"Sea otters have the thinnest fur of any aquatic mammal.","e":"Up to 1 million hairs per square inch — the densest fur on Earth, which is why they don't need blubber."}
]